From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Thu Oct 18 16:43:03 2018
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Thu, 18 Oct 2018 16:43:03 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] CAR as a linear combination
Message-ID: <REqz393e87o.TrfhVpPxBAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <REqz393e87o.UVTuqIEoBAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <REqz393e87o.MxpzovjSAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <REqz393e87o.UVTuqIEoBAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/REqz393e87o/m/TrfhVpPxBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Aha, so you want each beta to be a spatial CAR field of it's own, and
on top of that add CAR variability?
The same principle applies; the z-variability can have an f() of its
own (seems it's a BYM model you want for that, with CAR+independent
noise?), with zero mean, and each beta can have its own f():

For example,
  y ~ f(beta1, x1, model="besag") + f(beta2, x2, model="besag") + f(z,
model="bym")
with family="possion", so that E(y_i) = exp(eta_i) would define a
model where at each location,
 \eta_i = beta1_i x1_i + beta2_i x2_i + z_i

If the number of beta-fields is dynamic or large, that becomes tedious
to spell out, and you get one hyperparameter for each term as well,
but it seems to be what you're looking for.

Finn

