On Thursday, 18 October 2018 17:43:03 UTC+1, Finn Lindgren wrote:
> Aha, so you want each beta to be a spatial CAR field of it's own, and
> on top of that add CAR variability?

I'm confused, sorry. If each beta-field has a CAR structure it means that the latent field z is a sum of independent beta-fields, right? I'd like to consider correlation between them. What I'm trying is to conceive my latent field z as a "stochastic" piece-wise function (as exp(z_i) will be the rate over region i).

> The same principle applies; the z-variability can have an f() of its
> own (seems it's a BYM model you want for that, with CAR+independent
> noise?), with zero mean, and each beta can have its own f():
>
> For example,
>   y ~ f(beta1, x1, model="besag") + f(beta2, x2, model="besag") + f(z,
> model="bym")
> with family="possion", so that E(y_i) = exp(eta_i) would define a
> model where at each location,
>  \eta_i = beta1_i x1_i + beta2_i x2_i + z_i

This \eta_i looks what I want but I'm not sure in terms of variance. I'll do the maths to have a better understanding.

>
> If the number of beta-fields is dynamic or large, that becomes tedious
> to spell out, and you get one hyperparameter for each term as well,
> but it seems to be what you're looking for.

Fortunately, I'm working with scenarios where the number of betas varies between 2-5 and n=1480. So I'm hoping it will be computational feasible.

Thanks a lot again,
Roman.

